## Computing \(H_{dR}^k(S^1)\) via the Mayer–Vietoris Sequence

### Setup

Cover the circle \(S^1\) by two open arcs:
- \(U = S^1 \setminus \{N\}\) (circle minus the north pole),
- \(V = S^1 \setminus \{S\}\) (circle minus the south pole),

where \(N, S\) are distinct points. Both \(U\) and \(V\) are open arcs, hence **diffeomorphic to \(\mathbb{R}\)**, and their intersection
\[
U \cap V = S^1 \setminus \{N, S\}
\]
consists of **two disjoint open arcs**, hence is diffeomorphic to \(\mathbb{R} \sqcup \mathbb{R}\).

### Cohomology of the pieces

Since each piece is a disjoint union of contractible open arcs:

\[
H_{dR}^k(U) \cong \mathbb{R},\quad H_{dR}^k(V) \cong \mathbb{R},\qquad k = 0,
\]
\[
H_{dR}^k(U) = 0,\quad H_{dR}^k(V) = 0,\qquad k \geq 1.
\]

For the intersection (two components):
\[
H_{dR}^0(U\cap V) \cong \mathbb{R} \oplus \mathbb{R},\qquad H_{dR}^k(U\cap V) = 0,\quad k \geq 1.
\]

### The Mayer–Vietoris sequence

For de Rham cohomology, the Mayer–Vietoris sequence associated to \((U,V)\) is the long exact sequence

\[
0 \to H_{dR}^0(S^1) \xrightarrow{\;i^*\;} H_{dR}^0(U)\oplus H_{dR}^0(V) \xrightarrow{\;j^*\;} H_{dR}^0(U\cap V) \xrightarrow{\;d^*\;} H_{dR}^1(S^1) \xrightarrow{\;i^*\;} H_{dR}^1(U)\oplus H_{dR}^1(V) \to \cdots
\]

Substituting the computed groups:

\[
0 \to H_{dR}^0(S^1) \xrightarrow{\;i^*\;} \mathbb{R}\oplus\mathbb{R} \xrightarrow{\;j^*\;} \mathbb{R}\oplus\mathbb{R} \xrightarrow{\;d^*\;} H_{dR}^1(S^1) \xrightarrow{\;i^*\;} 0 \to \cdots
\]

### Degree 0

The map \(i^*: H_{dR}^0(S^1) \to H_{dR}^0(U)\oplus H_{dR}^0(V)\) sends a constant function \(c\) on \(S^1\) to \((c|_U, c|_V) = (c,c)\). This is injective, and its image is the diagonal \(\Delta = \{(c,c): c\in\mathbb{R}\} \subset \mathbb{R}\oplus\mathbb{R}\).

The map \(j^*: H_{dR}^0(U)\oplus H_{dR}^0(V) \to H_{dR}^0(U\cap V)\) sends \((a,b)\) (a constant \(a\) on \(U\), constant \(b\) on \(V\)) to the pair of restrictions \((a|_{U\cap V}, b|_{U\cap V})\) on the two components of \(U\cap V\). Writing the two components as \(C_1, C_2\), this is
\[
j^*(a,b) = (a, a,\, b, b) \in \mathbb{R}^4 \cong \mathbb{R}\oplus\mathbb{R}\oplus\mathbb{R}\oplus\mathbb{R},
\]
i.e. under the identification \(H_{dR}^0(U\cap V)\cong \mathbb{R}\oplus\mathbb{R}\) (one value per component, but each component carries both the \(U\)-restriction and \(V\)-restriction), the image of \(j^*\) is the diagonal \(\{(a,b,a,b)\}\) — equivalently, \(\ker j^* = \{(a,b): a=b\} = \Delta = \operatorname{im} i^*\).

By exactness at \(\mathbb{R}\oplus\mathbb{R}\) (the \(H_{dR}^0(U)\oplus H_{dR}^0(V)\) term):
\[
H_{dR}^0(S^1) \cong \ker j^* = \Delta \cong \mathbb{R}.
\]

So
\[
\boxed{H_{dR}^0(S^1) \cong \mathbb{R}}.
\]

### Degree 1

From exactness, \(\operatorname{im} j^* = \ker d^*\), and \(d^*: H_{dR}^0(U\cap V) \to H_{dR}^1(S^1)\) is surjective (since the next term \(H_{dR}^1(U)\oplus H_{dR}^1(V) = 0\)). Therefore
\[
H_{dR}^1(S^1) \cong H_{dR}^0(U\cap V)\,/\,\operatorname{im} j^*.
\]

Now \(H_{dR}^0(U\cap V) \cong \mathbb{R}\oplus\mathbb{R}\) (one constant per component of \(U\cap V\)). The image of \(j^*\) consists of pairs \((a,b)\in\mathbb{R}\oplus\mathbb{R}\) where the two components agree — but more precisely, \(j^*(a,b)\) restricted to the two components gives the same pair \((a,b)\) on both, so \(\operatorname{im} j^*\) is the **diagonal** \(\Delta = \{(a,a)\}\subset \mathbb{R}\oplus\mathbb{R}\) (the case where the constant on both components of \(U\cap V\) is the same, coming from a global constant).

Hence
\[
H_{dR}^1(S^1) \cong (\mathbb{R}\oplus\mathbb{R})\,/\,\Delta \cong \mathbb{R}.
\]

A concrete generator: the 1-form \(\omega = d\theta\) on \(S^1\) (the angular form) is closed but not exact (its integral over \(S^1\) is \(2\pi \neq 0\)), so it represents a nontrivial class generating \(H_{dR}^1(S^1)\).

So
\[
\boxed{H_{dR}^1(S^1) \cong \mathbb{R}}.
\]

### Higher degrees

For \(k \geq 2\), the Mayer–Vietoris sequence gives
\[
\cdots \to H_{dR}^{k-1}(U\cap V) \to H_{dR}^k(S^1) \to H_{dR}^k(U)\oplus H_{dR}^k(V) \to \cdots
\]
and all three groups are \(0\) (since \(U, V, U\cap V\) are disjoint unions of contractible arcs). Hence
\[
H_{dR}^k(S^1) = 0,\qquad k \geq 2.
\]

### Result

\[
H_{dR}^k(S^1) \cong
\begin{cases}
\mathbb{R}, & k = 0,\\[4pt]
\mathbb{R}, & k = 1,\\[4pt]
0, & k \geq 2.
\end{cases}
\]

